<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Benazir Bhutto</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1953">1953</a>–<a href="/years/2007">2007</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Benazir Bhutto was a Pakistani politician, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulfikar-ali-bhutto">Zulfikar Ali Bhutto</a> and leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> Peoples Party. She served as prime minister from 1988 to 1990 and from 1993 to 1996, the first woman to lead the government of a Muslim-majority country. Both governments were dismissed amid corruption charges. She was assassinated in Rawalpindi in December 2007 while campaigning after years of exile.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/benazir-bhutto.jpg" alt="Benazir Bhutto" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oliver_Mark_-_Benazir_Bhutto,_Dubai_2006_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Benazir Bhutto was born on 21 June 1953 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachi">Karachi</a>, the eldest of four children of the barrister and politician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zulfikar-ali-bhutto">Zulfikar Ali Bhutto</a> and Begum Nusrat Ispahani, who was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> of Persian and Kurdish descent. The Bhuttos were wealthy landlords from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>. From 1969 to 1973 she studied at Radcliffe College, entering at sixteen, and graduated cum laude in comparative government. She then studied at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, where she became President of the Oxford Union.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1977 her father was ousted in a military coup and later executed. Benazir and her mother took control of the Pakistan People's Party and led the Movement for the Restoration of Democracy. She was repeatedly imprisoned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-zia-ul-haq">Zia-ul-Haq</a>'s military government and lived in self-exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> from 1984, returning in 1986. She chaired the party from 1982 until her death and moved its platform from socialist to liberal. After Zia died in an air crash in August 1988, she led the party to victory and became Pakistan's first female prime minister.</p><p>Her attempts at reform were stifled by conservative and Islamist forces, including President Ghulam Ishaq Khan and the military, and her administration, accused of corruption and nepotism, was dismissed in 1990. She was sworn in again on 19 October 1993, after the party won 86 seats. In this term she established a Ministry of Women's Development and a Women's Development Bank and sought to privatise industries nationalised in the 1970s. Her brother Murtaza was killed, and on 5 November 1996 President Farooq Leghari dismissed her government on grounds of corruption and incompetence.</p><p>She lived in exile between Dubai and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> from 1998 and was convicted by a Swiss court in 2003. She returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> in October 2007, after negotiations with President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pervez-musharraf">Pervez Musharraf</a>, to contest the 2008 elections.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 27 December 2007 she spoke at a party rally in Rawalpindi's Liaquat National Bagh. As she left in a bulletproof vehicle and stood up through its escape hatch to wave, a man fired three shots and detonated a suicide vest. Twenty-two others also died. She was buried beside her father at Garhi Khuda Bakhsh. Al-Qaeda claimed responsibility, though other involvement was also suggested. Opinions of her were deeply divided: critics called her inexperienced and corrupt, and Islamist and conservative forces opposed her secularist, modernising agenda. After her death she came to be regarded as an icon for women's rights.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Benazir Bhutto” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Benazir Bhutto</b> became the first woman prime minister in 1988, and power alternated between her Pakistan Peoples Party and Nawaz Sharif's Pakistan Muslim League (N) until Musharraf's coup; the first democratic transition came in May 2013, Imran Khan's PTI won in 2018, and Shehbaz Sharif took office after…</blockquote><a href="/empire/islamic-republic-of-pakistan">Islamic Republic of Pakistan · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Pakistan Peoples Party, led by <b>Benazir Bhutto</b>, won the most seats in the first open election in over a decade.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-16">16 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachi">Karachi</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. Died in Benazir Bhutto Hospital, in what is now <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=24.86,67.01&amp;year=1988" aria-label="Karachi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Benazir Bhutto"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5543 5422h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5543 5422h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=24.86,67.01&amp;year=1988">What was here in 1988? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
